The Folder class does not expose Logger, FileSystem, or CollectionFolder as virtual or protected properties, so I cannot override or set them directly. Also, RefreshLinkedChildren is protected, so I cannot call it from outside.

Given these constraints, I cannot easily write a unit test that directly calls RefreshLinkedChildren and verifies the Logger.LogError call without modifying the production code.

Since apply_refactor to make RefreshLinkedChildren virtual failed due to missing Roslyn tool, and the members are not virtual or accessible, the best approach is to write a test subclass that exposes a public method calling RefreshLinkedChildren, and uses reflection to set the private fields or properties for Logger, FileSystem, and CollectionFolder to mocks.

I will write such a test now, using reflection to set private fields or properties named "Logger", "FileSystem", and "CollectionFolder" on Folder instance to mocks, then call the public wrapper method that calls RefreshLinkedChildren, and verify Logger.LogError calls.

